{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Phase 2 final debug report (repair verified; Gerrit landing pending)\n\nProblem: Gerrit 1876 PS2 failed only on Ubuntu/Python 3.11 after 13,892 passes because shared snapshot teardown recursively descended into a canonical tracker mkdir lock that vanished mid-walk.\n\nProven root cause: `tests/conftest.py::_remove_readonly_run_snapshots` used broad `Path.rglob` across the whole test `tmp_path`; supported Python 3.11 caches child entries and does not suppress `FileNotFoundError` when a cached directory disappears before descent. A real `LockHandle.release()` at the post-parent-scandir barrier reproduced the exact `.tickets-tracker/.ticket-write.lock.d` traceback in the actual fixture. Python 3.12 tolerated the identical interleaving. Direct exact-root cleanup succeeded while the real lock remained held, rejecting async MCP and canonical lock lifecycle as defects.\n\nRED/GREEN: the held-out oracle in `tests/unit/test_ci_snapshot_fixture_cleanup.py::test_snapshot_cleanup_tolerates_disappearing_tracker_lock` creates a real lock, exact snapshot tree, and adjacent file; releases the lock at a deterministic scandir barrier; and invokes the actual root fixture. Final-form Python 3.11 RED was the exact vanished-lock `FileNotFoundError`. The blind fixer never saw the oracle. With the repair it is GREEN, removes the snapshot root, and preserves adjacent bytes. A second control proves `PermissionError` remains visible. Reintroducing the original `Path.rglob` line returned the exact-defect mutation to RED; restoring the repair returned 2/2 GREEN. The oracle asserts observable teardown consequences and is insensitive to helper names/extraction.\n\nFix: recursive discovery now uses `os.walk(..., onerror=...)`, ignores only `FileNotFoundError` for concurrently vanished entries, re-raises every other walk error, retains arbitrary-depth exact `.rebar/run_snapshots` discovery, and keeps `_rmtree_writable`/read-only publication unchanged. No production code, dependency, workflow lifecycle, or store lock semantics changed; no existing assertion was weakened.\n\nVerification: Python 3.11 focused matrix 30 passed; Python 3.12 focused matrix 29 passed/1 expected version skip. Deterministic barrier passed 25/25 on Python 3.11. The original MCP workflow node passed 10/10 on a proper Python 3.11 environment. CI-shaped broad default gate (`-n 4 --dist worksteal`, non-integration/non-external/non-repo-policy) passed 13,886 with 53 skipped in 15:03. `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and `git diff --check` passed.\n\nCaller/safety sweep: the autouse fixture is the only behavioral caller. Existing snapshot producers at `tests/unit/workflow/test_snapshot.py`, `test_prior_art_hardening.py`, and the cross-module cleanup test depend on recursive exact-root coverage, which is preserved. The live tracker/lock and MCP async path are intended behavioral dependencies and were not changed.\n\nSibling sweep: an AST census of every pytest fixture containing a yield plus `rglob`/`glob`/`walk`/`scandir`, followed by teardown-hook/finalizer searches, found no other live recursive teardown scan; only this repaired fixture matched. Parity axis is N/A: this is a shared pytest lifecycle invariant, not a multi-interface operation.\n\nResidual risk/prevention: none observed without disposition. Python 3.11 carries the deterministic race oracle; later runtimes still execute the non-disappearance error control and the full shared cleanup suite.\n\nReplication: the original failing node no longer reproduced in 10 Python 3.11 runs, and the stronger synchronized lock-release scenario passed 25/25."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787096427949297001,"uuid":"99acdb84-fc7a-44d3-a342-de1323cfef64"}